[[:db/retract 17592186174490 :answer-field/choices 17592186174491] [:db/retract 17592186174490 :answer-field/choices 17592186174492] [:db/retract 17592186174490 :answer-field/choices 17592186174493] [:db/retract 17592186174490 :answer-field/choices 17592186174494] [:db/retract 17592186174490 :answer-field/choices 17592186174495] {:db/id 17592186176040 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ae249912-4f99-5805-8937-8f946d59caf8" "question-652b7852-9baf-5786-af1c-6a9b4e529ff9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175687 :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{10}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta)$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta.$ For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{10},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{10} x_i = 7.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Bernoulli random variable is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta^x (1-\\theta)^{1-x}, \\qquad x = 0,1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=10$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\theta^x(1-\\theta)^{1-x}, \\qquad x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =({\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ({\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{2}})\\,⋯\\,({\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (1-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i.$ \n\nWe are told that $\\displaystyle S=\\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i=7$ and $n=10.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{7}(1-\\theta )^{10-7} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{7}(1-\\theta )^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176062 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-43e2161d-2ae7-5d04-bc29-2934b939fe8c" "question-cec4340f-d49e-5a8e-a40d-7d35274dd0d2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175708 :question/problem "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{12}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim B(4,\\theta)$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta.$ For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{12},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{12} x_i = 22.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample up to a constant $C.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a binomial random variable is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} \\theta^x (1-\\theta)^{n-x}, \\qquad x = 0,1,2,\\ldots, n.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1 = x_1, X_2 = x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n = 12$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N = 4$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{4-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2,3,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (4-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,4n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{4n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle C = \\prod\\limits_{i=1}^n  {4 \\choose x_i}$ is a constant and $\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^{n} x_i.$\n\nWe are told that $\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^{n} x_i = 22$ and $n = 12.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{22}(1-\\theta )^{4(12)-22} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{22}(1-\\theta )^{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176063 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-83f5b615-5500-5815-8837-5144deae0691"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175709 :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,X_3,X_4,X_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\theta)$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta.$ Find the likelihood function of the sample $0, 2, 3, 1, 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i; \\theta).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\dfrac{\\theta^{x} e^{-\\theta}}{x!}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,\\ldots\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!})\\cdot (\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!})⋯(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle C=\\prod\\limits_{i=1}^{n} (x_i)!$ is a constant and $\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0+2+3+1+1 \\\\  & =7 \\\\ C & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})! \\\\  & =0!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 3!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 1! \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot 6\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{12}\\,{\\theta }^{7}\\,{e}^{-5\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-ae249912-4f99-5805-8937-8f946d59caf8" :question/id #uuid "ae249912-4f99-5805-8937-8f946d59caf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-159812" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta )$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{12},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}=7.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Bernoulli random variable is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=12$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =({\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ({\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{2}})\\,⋯\\,({\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (1-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=7$ and $n=12.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{7}(1-\\theta )^{12-7} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{7}(1-\\theta )^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-85ffbc3d-45e9-55be-b3f3-ac28c599fe38"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-85ffbc3d-45e9-55be-b3f3-ac28c599fe38" :answer-field/id #uuid "85ffbc3d-45e9-55be-b3f3-ac28c599fe38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4e09edd5-