heta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=15$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =({\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ({\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{2}})\\,⋯\\,({\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (1-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=10$ and $n=15.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{10}(1-\\theta )^{15-10} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{10}(1-\\theta )^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2f604128-dde5-5878-8593-fd5ec0ff3efc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2f604128-dde5-5878-8593-fd5ec0ff3efc" :answer-field/id #uuid "2f604128-dde5-5878-8593-fd5ec0ff3efc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c09dcbe2-1249-5a2a-a814-42f3694504c1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c364aca3-b3e0-5c08-8833-f92f2c90e923" "answer-c09dcbe2-1249-5a2a-a814-42f3694504c1" "answer-733053ef-496c-512e-b472-26d977bfe224" "answer-1c1b5d67-2015-546a-b753-e86796910bb3" "answer-e346e105-c8a1-5024-ace6-a389edb0c7db"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c364aca3-b3e0-5c08-8833-f92f2c90e923" :answer/id #uuid "c364aca3-b3e0-5c08-8833-f92f2c90e923" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\theta }^{10}(1-\\theta )^{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c09dcbe2-1249-5a2a-a814-42f3694504c1" :answer/id #uuid "c09dcbe2-1249-5a2a-a814-42f3694504c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\theta }^{10}(1-\\theta )^{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-733053ef-496c-512e-b472-26d977bfe224" :answer/id #uuid "733053ef-496c-512e-b472-26d977bfe224" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\theta }^{15}(1-\\theta )^{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c1b5d67-2015-546a-b753-e86796910bb3" :answer/id #uuid "1c1b5d67-2015-546a-b753-e86796910bb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\theta }^{5}(1-\\theta )^{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e346e105-c8a1-5024-ace6-a389edb0c7db" :answer/id #uuid "e346e105-c8a1-5024-ace6-a389edb0c7db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\theta }^{5}(1-\\theta )^{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-43e2161d-2ae7-5d04-bc29-2934b939fe8c" :question/id #uuid "43e2161d-2ae7-5d04-bc29-2934b939fe8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-159817" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{8}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼B(3,\\theta )$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{8},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}=16.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample up to a constant $C.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a binomial random variable is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{n-x},\\,x=0,1,2,…,n.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=8$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=3$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{3-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{3-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{3-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{3-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{3-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (3-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,3n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{3n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}})$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=16$ and $n=8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{16}(1-\\theta )^{3(8)-16} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{16}(1-\\theta )^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-923e314c-9d30-5ad4-a1e8-be7e907b3953"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-923e314c-9d30-5ad4-a1e8-be7e907b3953" :answer-field/id #uuid "923e314c-9d30-5ad4-a1e8-be7e907b3953" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a69f56a5-8274-5d79-86ae-2334c516061c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b25bf37a-582a-55f4-ac43-30454c204ab3" "answer-a69f56a5-8274-5d79-86ae-2334c516061c" "answer-72aacfd9-f2cf-5f0b-b080-dad3ce66cc66" "answer-29052ac4-071e-54fb-ae16-82067d8037d0" "answer-4b5112df-b8a5-508d-bd8d-84e15497dc00"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b25bf37a-582a-55f4-ac43-30454c204ab3" :answer/id #uuid "b25bf37a-582a-55f4-ac43-30454c204ab3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{16}(1-\\theta )^{24}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a69f56a5-8274-5d79-86ae-2334c516061c" :answer/id #uuid "a69f56a5-8274-5d79-86ae-2334c516061c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{16}(1-\\theta )^{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72aacfd9-f2cf-5f0b-b080-dad3ce66cc66" :answer/id #uuid "72aacfd9-f2cf-5f0b-b080-dad3ce66cc66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{24}(1-\\theta )^{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29052ac4-071e-54fb-ae16-82067d8037d0" :answer/id #uuid "29052ac4-071e-54fb-ae16-82067d8037d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{24}(1-\\theta )^{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b5112df-b8a5-508d-bd8d-84e15497dc00" :answer/id #uuid "4b5112df-b8a5-508d-bd8d-84e15497dc00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{8}(1-\\theta )^{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cec4340f-d49e-5a8e-a40d-7d35274dd0d2" :question/id #uuid "cec4340f-d49e-5a8e-a40d-7d35274dd0d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-159818" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{14}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼B(6,\\theta )$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{14},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{14}{x}_{i}=24.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample up to a constant $C.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a binomial random variable is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{n-x},\\,x=0,1,2,…,n.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=14$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=6$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{6-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2,…,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{6-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{6-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{6-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{6-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (6-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,6n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{6n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}})$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=24$ and $n=14.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{24}(1-\\theta )^{6(14)-24} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{24}(1-\\theta )^{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7b2cde9e-b33c-5092-8ba3-4c4a250a40a0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7b2cde9e-b33c-5092-8ba3-4c4a250a40a0" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b2cde9e-b33c-5092-8ba3-4c4a250a40a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-410f39b6-a584-5d06-bfad-158d3f61cf1c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-410f39b6-a584-5d06-bfad-158d3f61cf1c" "answer-71541f16-f169-5d86-b0ba-8063949a0c45" "answer-dd08ec01-50e9-52c6-8a79-0d3ad5f4d4fe" "answer-389582af-a58f-54a7-ae30-7b904bf70c5c" "answer-74f9697a-0180-5e52-8045-a99af5573240"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-410f39b6-a584-5d06-bfad-158d3f61cf1c" :answer/id #uuid "410f39b6-a584-5d06-bfad-158d3f61cf1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{24}(1-\\theta )^{60}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71541f16-f169-5d86-b0ba-8063949a0c45" :answer/id #uuid "71541f16-f169-5d86-b0ba-8063949a0c45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{24}(1-\\theta )^{84}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd08ec01-50e9-52c6-8a79-0d3ad5f4d4fe" :answer/id #uuid "dd08ec01-50e9-52c6-8a79-0d3ad5f4d4fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{60}(1-\\theta )^{24}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-389582af-a58f-54a7-ae30-7b904bf70c5c" :answer/id #uuid "389582af-a58f-54a7-ae30-7b904bf70c5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{84}(1-\\theta )^{24}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74f9697a-0180-5e52-8045-a99af5573240" :answer/id #uuid "74f9697a-0180-5e52-8045-a99af5573240" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{84}(1-\\theta )^{60}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-83f5b615-5500-5815-8837-5144deae0691" :question/id #uuid "83f5b615-5500-5815-8837-5144deae0691" :question/math-academy-id "q-159827" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{17}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{17},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{17}{x}_{i}=8,\\,{\\prod }_{i=1}^{17}({x}_{i})!=4.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$\n\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=17$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!})\\cdot (\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!})⋯(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=8,$ $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!=4,$ and $n=17.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{\\theta }^{8}\\,{e}^{-17\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-75347981-9a28-5cb6-b027-b092523fc953"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-75347981-9a28-5cb6-b027-b092523fc953" :answer-field/id #uuid "75347981-9a28-5cb6-b027-b092523fc953" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-deae0969-c9d6-5654-be64-6233a00a8a9b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-43ce76c4-9b3f-5e0d-8d4b-7dd6c7eb8a9b" "answer-8a6185d9-5090-58f9-9faf-f0ca13d18ad0" "answer-a64a48b1-2e38-5fa2-80c1-00ca67502114" "answer-deae0969-c9d6-5654-be64-6233a00a8a9b" "answer-f9f696ee-8ecd-5fda-b688-f8cccd8853c8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43ce76c4-9b3f-5e0d-8d4b-7dd6c7eb8a9b" :answer/id #uuid "43ce76c4-9b3f-5e0d-8d4b-7dd6c7eb8a9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,{\\theta }^{8}\\,{e}^{-2\\theta }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a6185d9-5090-58f9-9faf-f0ca13d18ad0" :answer/id #uuid "8a6185d9-5090-58f9-9faf-f0ca13d18ad0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\,{\\theta }^{17}\\,{e}^{-8\\theta }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a64a48b1-2e38-5fa2-80c1-00ca67502114" :answer/id #uuid "a64a48b1-2e38-5fa2-80c1-00ca67502114" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,{\\theta }^{2}\\,{e}^{-17\\theta }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-deae0969-c9d6-5654-be64-6233a00a8a9b" :answer/id #uuid "deae0969-c9d6-5654-be64-6233a00a8a9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,{\\theta }^{8}\\,{e}^{-17\\theta }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9f696ee-8ecd-5fda-b688-f8cccd8853c8" :answer/id #uuid "f9f696ee-8ecd-5fda-b688-f8cccd8853c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\,{\\theta }^{2}\\,{e}^{-8\\theta }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176372 :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{16},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{16}{x}_{i}=18,\\,{\\prod }_{i=1}^{16}({x}_{i})!=6.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=16$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!})\\cdot (\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!})⋯(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=18,$ $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!=6,$ and $n=16.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{6}\\,{\\theta }^{18}\\,{e}^{-16\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186174490 :answer-field/correct "answer-e63ce25c-9077-5101-b2f1-edcd452a4672" :answer-field/choices ["answer-838c6fad-c2ba-572a-946c-b271606a25e2" "answer-638f95eb-fe03-5c9c-b325-ab64d1a2bfb8" "answer-c60c1729-d011-5a55-ab06-3140f2535dae" "answer-e63ce25c-9077-5101-b2f1-edcd452a4672" "answer-da146878-2071-56de-b976-f02f6e1c86b4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-838c6fad-c2ba-572a-946c-b271606a25e2" :answer/id #uuid "838c6fad-c2ba-572a-946c-b271606a25e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\,{\\theta }^{16}\\,{e}^{-18\\theta }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-638f95eb-fe03-5c9c-b325-ab64d1a2bfb8" :answer/id #uuid "638f95eb-fe03-5c9c-b325-ab64d1a2bfb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\,{\\theta }^{18}\\,{e}^{-2\\theta }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c60c1729-d011-5a55-ab06-3140f2535dae" :answer/id #uuid "c60c1729-d011-5a55-ab06-3140f2535dae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\,{\\theta }^{2}\\,{e}^{-18\\theta }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e63ce25c-9077-5101-b2f1-edcd452a4672" :answer/id #uuid "e63ce25c-9077-5101-b2f1-edcd452a4672" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\,{\\theta }^{18}\\,{e}^{-16\\theta }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da146878-2071-56de-b976-f02f6e1c86b4" :answer/id #uuid "da146878-2071-56de-b976-f02f6e1c86b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\,{\\theta }^{2}\\,{e}^{-16\\theta }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176991 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we might know the general form of the probability distribution for a population, yet we may not know some of its underlying parameters. We wish to develop strategies to estimate unknown population parameters from sample data. One such strategy is to use **likelihood functions.** \n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to compute likelihood functions for particular discrete probability distributions.\n\nSuppose we have an I.I.D. (independent and identically distributed) random sample\n\n$$\nX_1, \\: X_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: X_n,\n$$\n\nwhere the probability mass function of each $X_i$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x;\\,\\theta)\n$$\n\nand $\\theta$ is an *unknown,* fixed parameter. \n\nSuppose we have a particular sample from this distribution:\n\n$$\nx_1, \\: x_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: x_n\n$$\n\nWe define the **likelihood function** $L(\\theta)$ as \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n).\n$$\n\nThe likelihood function gives the probability of getting a particular sample for a specific parameter value. \n\nNow, since we assumed that all $X_i$'s are independent and identically distributed, by the multiplication law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n}) \\\\  & =P({X}_{1}={x}_{1})\\cdot P({X}_{2}={x}_{2})⋯P({X}_{n}={x}_{n}) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}P({X}_{i}={x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, the likelihood function $L(\\theta)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nLet's take a look at a concrete example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186177074 :tutorial/content "Suppose that \n\n$$\nX_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad X_3,\\quad X_4,\\quad X_5,\\quad X_6\n$$\n\n \n\nis an I.I.D random sample from a population, where \n\n$$\nX_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta)\n$$\n\nand $\\theta$ is the *unknown* probability of success. \n\nSuppose we conduct a sample and get the following data:\n\n$$\nx_1=0, \\quad x_2=1, \\quad x_3=1, \\quad x_4=0, \\quad x_5=1, \\quad x_6=1\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's compute the likelihood function for this sample.\n\nRecall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are I.I.D random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nThe probability mass function for a Bernoulli random variable with an unknown probability of success $\\theta$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\theta^x(1-\\theta)^{1-x}, \\qquad x=0,1.\n$$\n\nUsing this definition, we can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =({\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ({\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{2}})\\,⋯\\,({\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (1-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i.$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that $n=6,$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0+1+1+0+1+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the likelihood function for this sample is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{4}(1-\\theta )^{6-4} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{4}(1-\\theta )^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $L(\\theta)$ is shown below. \n\n![](images/28/28c73749a566d5531ef2f13c5b01bd1d26ced28e19749abf056841492fb497f5.png)\n\nNotice that $L(\\theta)$ has a maximum inside the interval $\\theta \\in [0,1].$ The value $\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}$ that corresponds to the maximum likelihood is called the **maximum likelihood estimate** of the parameter $\\theta$ for the given sample. \n\nWe'll discuss how to compute the maximum likelihood estimate in future topics. But, for now, let's see more examples of finding the likelihood functions for different distributions and samples." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176143 :activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
